00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.120
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.020
We've been having a very rich debate over
the past few days internally between both

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.590
classes and

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:37.350
drawing the line between our personal
reactions to what as an institution

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:42.960
we need to represent, has been a tricky,
I'll say at least, discussion.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.830
So thank you very much for
this perspective, it's great.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.660
And before we go into the specifics of
LinkedIn, I wanted to get your take on

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:53.910
another topic that could potentially
have very high impact on the workforce.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.170
So a couple of months ago you
addressed the LinkedIn employees and

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:03.140
you told them about what some people refer
to as the fourth industrial revolution.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.290
So can you tell us a bit more about that
concept and how you think it's actually

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.100
going to shift the dynamics of
the workforce and the economy as well?

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:14.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, so
this was a theory that

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.200
I first became familiar with
through the World Economic Forum,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.550
they were talking about
the fourth industrial revolution.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.610
The first, mechanization,
largely based on the steam engine.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:32.100
And the second was mass production,
and that was largely based on

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.691
the introduction of electricity, division
of labor, assembly lines and factories.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.968
The third was automation and that followed
electronics, early stage computing and IT.

00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:49.860
And this fourth industrial
revolution based on robotization.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.980
And the technologies and

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:58.287
the innovations underpinning this fourth
industrial revolution includes robots.

00:07:58.287 --> 00:08:03.209
AI, sensors, autonomous driving, etc.

00:08:03.209 --> 00:08:08.490
And what's noteworthy about this
Fourth Industrial Revolution is that,

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:13.768
in the first three cases, new innovations,
new technologies gave rise to

00:08:13.768 --> 00:08:20.620
companies that generated massive gains in
productivity and new job opportunities.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.490
And there is increasingly
a belief that in this

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.399
latest Fourth Industrial Revolution
that may not be the case.

00:08:27.399 --> 00:08:31.386
That the new technologies may
actually displace more workers and

00:08:31.386 --> 00:08:32.844
jobs than they create.

00:08:32.844 --> 00:08:33.709
And certainly,

00:08:33.709 --> 00:08:37.785
that was the case in this body of research
from the World Economic Forum, where

00:08:37.785 --> 00:08:42.701
they predicted, well, 7 million gross jobs
would be eliminated between now and 2020.

00:08:42.701 --> 00:08:46.496
2 million would be added through
the advent of new technologies,

00:08:46.496 --> 00:08:48.401
so we would lose 5 million jobs.

00:08:48.401 --> 00:08:51.640
And there are some that believe that
is a highly conservative estimate.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:54.831
That more people than that will
be displaced as a result of new

00:08:54.831 --> 00:08:58.723
technologies and innovation and
you can see this now and start to feel it.

00:08:58.723 --> 00:09:02.340
And sure enough, at this year's
Davos event that's hosted by

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:06.641
the World Economic Forum, there was
a general consensus that one of the key

00:09:06.641 --> 00:09:10.668
points to come out of this one,
the key learnings was this whole meme,

00:09:10.668 --> 00:09:13.699
this whole idea,
has been discussed now for years.

00:09:13.699 --> 00:09:15.197
It's been predicted for years.

00:09:15.197 --> 00:09:19.574
And if you talked to attendees this year,
they felt like something had tipped.

00:09:19.574 --> 00:09:23.174
And whether it was heads of state or
CEOs, founders,

00:09:23.174 --> 00:09:27.979
there was a general consensus that
it was no longer a prognostication.

00:09:27.979 --> 00:09:29.662
It was actually happening.

00:09:29.662 --> 00:09:33.319
And all of you have probably seen
this anecdotally, week in and

00:09:33.319 --> 00:09:36.620
week out,
if you're following business news.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.413
It seems like literally every other week,

00:09:39.413 --> 00:09:44.068
there's another story about a major
factory that has decided to deploy

00:09:44.068 --> 00:09:48.429
robot technology that will displace
tens of thousands of people.

00:09:48.429 --> 00:09:53.089
Or a major retailer and
major warehousing infrastructure that has

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:57.594
grown the number of robots being
deployed by tens of thousands.

00:09:57.594 --> 00:10:01.799
Or autonomous driving which has the
potential to happen far faster than anyone

00:10:01.799 --> 00:10:03.900
could have anticipated.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.180
And for those that don't know,

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.710
trucking, it's the number one
profession in California.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.730
A lot of people don't realize that.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.310
And what happens to those folks?

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:20.560
I think it's absolutely essential
that any time there are stories about

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:25.480
these new technologies, it's wonderful
to be excited about innovation and

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:30.090
things that could potentially unlock tons
of value on a global basis for people.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.960
But I think it's also equally important,
if not more important,

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:37.240
every time we see these articles to
understand what's happening to the people

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.450
who are being impacted and
potentially displaced.

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:44.884
What programs are we putting into
place to reskill these employees?

00:10:44.884 --> 00:10:48.420
Are they being certified in new skills for
the jobs that are and

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.004
will be as opposed to
the jobs that once were?

00:10:51.004 --> 00:10:54.400
Where is the spirit of
entrepreneurship and

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.805
the investment and
the energy in vocational training and

00:10:58.805 --> 00:11:04.218
innovation in how we train people to
take on these new technologies and

00:11:04.218 --> 00:11:08.829
the job opportunities created
by these new technologies?

00:11:08.829 --> 00:11:13.678
How are we leveraging infrastructure
that we couldn't have even dreamed of

00:11:13.678 --> 00:11:14.596
decades ago?

00:11:14.596 --> 00:11:17.337
Take LinkedIn as an example,
when you look at the data we have,

00:11:17.337 --> 00:11:19.825
we're developing the world's
first economic graph and

00:11:19.825 --> 00:11:22.072
our understanding take any
locality in the world.

00:11:22.072 --> 00:11:25.767
And we're in a position now where we can
understand the fastest growing jobs within

00:11:25.767 --> 00:11:28.425
that locality,
the skills required to obtain those jobs.

00:11:28.425 --> 00:11:32.187
We can understand the skills of the
aggregate workforce within that locality.

00:11:32.187 --> 00:11:36.416
We can measure the gap and then we can
provide this data to junior colleges,

00:11:36.416 --> 00:11:40.247
vocational training facilities,
even four year universities and

00:11:40.247 --> 00:11:42.817
help them to create just
in time curriculum.

00:11:42.817 --> 00:11:46.463
This is the kind of thing, I think,
increasingly as a society,

00:11:46.463 --> 00:11:50.254
we need to be spending more time
on understanding and solving for.

00:11:50.254 --> 00:11:54.055
&gt;&gt; All right, so actually you've touched
the point, I was about to ask you next,

00:11:54.055 --> 00:11:58.540
which is educational reform is something
that you're very passionate about.

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:02.898
So let me rephrase the question and ask
are there any systems out there that you

00:12:02.898 --> 00:12:06.453
think will provide the better
match to the shifting economy and

00:12:06.453 --> 00:12:08.953
the needs of the workforce moving forward?

00:12:08.953 --> 00:12:13.497
&gt;&gt; Well, coming back to education
reform because it goes well beyond how

00:12:13.497 --> 00:12:18.196
we're going to reskill displaced workers,
I think from a generational

00:12:18.196 --> 00:12:22.837
perspective it starts with primary
education and then K through 12.

00:12:22.837 --> 00:12:28.450
And we need to make sure that
increasingly, we are providing schools and

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:33.510
teachers the assets, the curriculum,
the technology they need

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:38.307
to bring our students into
the 21st century successfully.

00:12:38.307 --> 00:12:43.427
Rote learning and the same things that
were deployed to help people train for

00:12:43.427 --> 00:12:48.067
the industrial revolutions that
came prior, that we talked about.

00:12:48.067 --> 00:12:52.446
For those who don't know, well,
we'll do a little bit of pop quiz.

00:12:52.446 --> 00:12:57.870
How many in the audience know why there
is summer vacation in American schools?

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:01.438
&gt;&gt; So that kids could go harvest.

00:13:01.438 --> 00:13:03.482
&gt;&gt; So
the kids could go harvest the fields.

00:13:03.482 --> 00:13:06.686
When was the last time any of you
went out harvesting the fields?

00:13:06.686 --> 00:13:10.953
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, do you know why in part,

00:13:10.953 --> 00:13:14.790
we sit in rows and
why rote learning was such

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:19.710
an important part of primary school,
anyone, yeah?

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.510
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, assembly lines, so

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:25.420
we exist in a different world and

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:29.333
increasingly the world has become
more digital more networked.

00:13:29.333 --> 00:13:31.910
Things change much faster
than they've ever changed.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:33.547
That's not anecdotal, it's empirical.

00:13:33.547 --> 00:13:38.238
And so,
we need to teach critical reasoning.

00:13:38.238 --> 00:13:40.950
We need to teach creative problem solving.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:42.653
We need to teach collaboration.

00:13:42.653 --> 00:13:45.022
We need to teach compassion.

00:13:45.022 --> 00:13:50.944
We need to change the way in which
we're training the future workforce.

00:13:50.944 --> 00:13:55.819
I think there are now technologies
that we could take advantage of,

00:13:55.819 --> 00:14:00.351
that will help teachers and
help administrators focus more and

00:14:00.351 --> 00:14:03.614
more of their time on
teaching these skills.

00:14:03.614 --> 00:14:07.731
And we can leverage adaptive
learning platforms to teach more of

00:14:07.731 --> 00:14:10.637
the commodity-based skills, the basics.

00:14:10.637 --> 00:14:15.241
And we're still in very early innings in
terms of being able to fully deploy and

00:14:15.241 --> 00:14:17.836
take advantage of these new technologies.

00:14:17.836 --> 00:14:21.537
We were talking a little bit about
the importance of vocational trainings, so

00:14:21.537 --> 00:14:24.959
moving beyond the generational change,
that's going to take years, but

00:14:24.959 --> 00:14:27.500
we gotta start investing
in that right now.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:30.015
But what about the workers
being displaced today?

00:14:30.015 --> 00:14:34.137
We need to go all in on
vocational training.

00:14:34.137 --> 00:14:39.683
And historically, and speaking of in
the US now from a societal perspective,

00:14:39.683 --> 00:14:43.127
I think,
we've placed tremendous emphasis and

00:14:43.127 --> 00:14:49.870
weighting on degrees from prestigious four
year universities or graduate schools.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.044
And that matters and it's important.

00:14:52.044 --> 00:14:57.404
But it's also important for
people who are going down a different

00:14:57.404 --> 00:15:03.155
path to have access to an education,
where they can develop a skill and

00:15:03.155 --> 00:15:08.926
a trade that they can utilize to earn
a living and create a career path.

00:15:08.926 --> 00:15:14.360
And you see this in countries like Germany
that have done this extraordinarily well.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:20.771
And so this isn't just a technical issue,
it's also a societal and a cultural issue.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:23.293
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that.

00:15:23.293 --> 00:15:25.611
Now, we want to get to know
you a little bit better, so

00:15:25.611 --> 00:15:29.006
we're going to take you a little bit back
to the start of your time at LinkedIn.

00:15:29.006 --> 00:15:30.352
&gt;&gt; I thought we were about to do,
this is your life.

00:15:30.352 --> 00:15:31.385
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:15:31.385 --> 00:15:33.510
they start flashing
pictures from my childhood.

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:35.692
&gt;&gt; That's what I would have done,
if immigration wasn't an issue there.

00:15:35.692 --> 00:15:36.853
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay.

00:15:36.853 --> 00:15:40.898
&gt;&gt; But essentially, back when you look
at when you first joined LinkedIn.

00:15:40.898 --> 00:15:41.898
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:15:41.898 --> 00:15:43.159
&gt;&gt; Transitions are very difficult,

00:15:43.159 --> 00:15:45.251
especially when it's a very
fast growing environment.

00:15:45.251 --> 00:15:45.832
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:15:45.832 --> 00:15:49.146
&gt;&gt; So what were the key success factors
that kind of made the hand over

00:15:49.146 --> 00:15:51.384
between Reid Hoffman and yourself smooth?

00:15:51.384 --> 00:15:53.601
&gt;&gt; Reid Hoffman,
Stanford alum Reid Hoffman?

00:15:53.601 --> 00:15:54.811
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:15:54.811 --> 00:15:58.574
&gt;&gt; Symbolic systems, anyone,
symbolic systems that you're under in?

00:15:58.574 --> 00:15:59.816
&gt;&gt; I had him in a class
last year as a guest.

00:15:59.816 --> 00:16:00.582
&gt;&gt; Did you?

00:16:00.582 --> 00:16:01.278
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:01.278 --> 00:16:06.343
Good man, very, very bright,
extraordinarily thoughtful,

00:16:06.343 --> 00:16:11.520
and that's actually a part of why
our hand off was so successful.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:19.030
So I first met Reid,
as the Dean said, at a dinner.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:23.770
We were at a restaurant in San Francisco
and we were doing some brainstorming for

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:27.440
an event we titled Brainstorm and
they wanted to talk to about 20 or

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:32.610
30 folks from the Valley about their
ideas for event material and the agenda.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:35.470
And I remember Reid answering
a question that's been posed, and

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:39.380
I was really struck by not
only his intelligence but

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:41.330
how he was coming at it from
a different perspective.

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:46.143
And we have been told by different people
who knew us that we should meet at some

00:16:46.143 --> 00:16:46.672
point.

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:49.337
So we had a chance to
meet on that occasion.

00:16:49.337 --> 00:16:53.857
And little piece of trivia,
Reid was actually the last guest speaker I

00:16:53.857 --> 00:16:57.762
brought in to talk to my team
when I was an executive at Yahoo,

00:16:57.762 --> 00:17:00.510
before I ended up leaving the company.

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:05.470
At any rate, when LinkedIn elected
to make a change at the CEO level,

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:10.140
for those that don't know,
Reid had been the founder and CEO.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:15.470
He had then hired a professional CEO, a
guy named Dan Nye who did a wonderful job.

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:21.170
And Reid was the president of product,
reporting to Dan who was the CEO,

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:25.845
who then reported to Reid as
the chairman and founder of the company.

00:17:25.845 --> 00:17:28.543
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What could possibly go

00:17:28.543 --> 00:17:30.326
wrong with that construct?

00:17:30.326 --> 00:17:32.575
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:17:32.575 --> 00:17:36.855
it didn't work out
the way people had hoped.

00:17:36.855 --> 00:17:39.450
And the two of them had a great
deal of admiration for one another.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:40.526
That's not what it was about.

00:17:40.526 --> 00:17:45.460
So when Reid was looking to make a change
and he asked me to get involved, he said,

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:49.525
I was at the time, I was an executive
in residency at Greylock, and

00:17:49.525 --> 00:17:51.723
LinkedIn was a portfolio company.

00:17:51.723 --> 00:17:53.496
And I understood they were making
a change and someone said,

00:17:53.496 --> 00:17:54.620
would I be interested in helping out?

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:55.370
I said absolutely.

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.180
That's one of the reasons I'm here.

00:17:57.180 --> 00:18:00.499
So I'll take a couple of days a week and
I'll help Reid out in any way I can.

00:18:00.499 --> 00:18:02.380
And he said, okay,
you should go meet with him.

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:02.880
I said okay.

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:05.320
So I go and meet with Reid.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:07.070
We were actually in his house.

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:12.720
And I think I was sitting in a LinkedIn
bean bag chair with the words innovation,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.280
and everything had an In logo on it.

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:17.850
And he said, so I have two lists.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.400
I have an interim CEO list and
a full time CEO list, and

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:22.640
you're the only person on both lists.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:23.862
So what do you say?

00:18:23.862 --> 00:18:24.541
I was like, whoa!

00:18:24.541 --> 00:18:25.557
Whoa, what?

00:18:25.557 --> 00:18:27.810
I was going to volunteer to help.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:32.410
He's like, no, no, you gotta come in and
let's make this happen.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:37.120
So I agreed that I was going to
start on an interim basis

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.860
because I had just left Yahoo.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:41.716
I was there for a number of years, and

00:18:41.716 --> 00:18:45.855
I wasn't sure how quickly I wanted to
get back into a full time position.

00:18:45.855 --> 00:18:47.805
And I said as long as he was
open to having me full time and

00:18:47.805 --> 00:18:49.275
I could start interim,
I'd be open to that.

00:18:49.275 --> 00:18:51.190
And he said as often as you're
open to being full time,

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:52.825
you'll start as interim and
then we'll see how it goes.

00:18:52.825 --> 00:18:53.325
I said great.

00:18:54.365 --> 00:18:56.235
The night before I joined, I called him.

00:18:56.235 --> 00:18:59.665
I was going to start as interim president
with all the responsibilities of a CEO,

00:18:59.665 --> 00:19:00.425
but interim president.

00:19:00.425 --> 00:19:01.095
And I called him and

00:19:01.095 --> 00:19:05.510
said so, you're still going to be the CEO,
at least in title symbolically.

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:06.700
I'm coming in as interim president.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.680
Which decisions would you like to make and

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.910
which decisions do you think
I should be responsible for?

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:13.440
I want to make sure we had
clarity before I started.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.150
He said, this is easy.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.590
It's your ball, you run with it.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.940
I go, what?

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:22.900
He said, yeah,
you're going to make all the decisions.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:26.940
That's why I'm bringing you in like this,
and I just learned what not to do.

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:34.450
Now to give you an indication of truly how
thoughtful he is, he went a step further.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.655
And over the course of the first
ten weeks I was at LinkedIn,

00:19:37.655 --> 00:19:41.530
Reid was out of the office,
he scheduled trips and travel, for

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:44.020
roughly six to eight weeks of that time.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:49.390
Because he recognized, no matter what we
explained to people, that muscle memory

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:52.370
was still going to be in place and
people were still going to go to him.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.860
So he literally physically took
himself out of the situation so

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:01.250
I could build that connective
tissue with the organization.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:06.024
So between that and between
the graciousness of my predecessor,

00:20:06.024 --> 00:20:09.162
Dan Nein, the way he handed off the baton.

00:20:09.162 --> 00:20:10.558
In a situation like that,

00:20:10.558 --> 00:20:14.500
the person who's leaving the company
can sometimes be angry and bitter.

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:17.760
And they can poison
the well to some extent.

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:20.470
And Dan did the exact opposite.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:24.030
He operated with absolute integrity
throughout the entire process.

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:28.540
The last thing I would say is one of
the most frequently asked questions I

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:32.690
got when I first joined LinkedIn was,
what's it like working with Reid?

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:36.870
And that was code for what's all
the drama happening between you and

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:38.850
the founder of the company?

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.673
And what people didn't realize
was I joined LinkedIn,

00:20:42.673 --> 00:20:46.510
not in spite of Reid, but
in large part because of Reid.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:50.260
I had a tremendous amount of respect and
admiration for him.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:52.320
I wanted to work with him.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:55.233
And he's gone on to become
one of my closest friends,

00:20:55.233 --> 00:20:57.647
someone I would certainly go to for
advice.

00:20:57.647 --> 00:21:01.420
And we've had a wonderful
partnership ever since.

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:01.994
&gt;&gt; Well, you've built a great thing.

00:21:01.994 --> 00:21:02.929
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:21:02.929 --> 00:21:06.630
&gt;&gt; And
you stress on purpose-driven culture.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.389
So what is the purpose of LinkedIn and

00:21:08.389 --> 00:21:11.702
how did that translate to the culture
that you guys built over there?

00:21:11.702 --> 00:21:16.054
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I mentioned earlier our
vision to create economic opportunity for

00:21:16.054 --> 00:21:18.330
every member of the global workforce.

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:22.030
We separate vision and
mission at LinkedIn.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:26.000
Some companies use these synonymously,
and I think that's a lost opportunity.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:27.105
Vision for us is the dream.

00:21:27.105 --> 00:21:27.625
It's true north.

00:21:27.625 --> 00:21:31.040
It gets us out of bed every morning,
coming in to work.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:35.070
Mission is an overarching objective for

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:41.580
the organization that is realizable,
measurable, and hopefully inspirational.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.620
So they play two different roles.

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:47.410
Our mission is to connect the world's
professionals to make them more productive

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:48.060
and successful.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.440
We believe deeply in our mission.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.360
We believe deeply in our vision.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:59.240
And our sense of purpose is one of the
things that defines us as an organization.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.860
It's not just the what, however.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.370
It's also the how, our culture and values.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:07.030
It's not just about what were
trying to accomplish and

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:08.170
the results that we generate.

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:10.600
It's how we go about doing that.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:15.320
And increasingly, I think both purpose and
the idea of culture and

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:19.360
values is what attracts
people to join organizations.

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:22.920
That's certainly the case with the younger
generation, with the millennial

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:27.980
generation, where you find time and
time again people seem to be more driven

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:35.090
by purpose than they do some of the things
that mattered perhaps more historically.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:38.220
Compensation, title,
these things are still important.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:40.684
But I think talking to more and
more younger people,

00:22:40.684 --> 00:22:44.492
purpose and what the company is ultimately
trying to accomplish, and the impact

00:22:44.492 --> 00:22:47.868
it's going to have in the world,
that seems to be more heavily weighted.

00:22:47.868 --> 00:22:50.140
And it's not just among employees.

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:53.980
It's also increasingly customers who,
by virtue of social media,

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:57.270
have a transparent view
into how companies operate.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:00.480
And increasingly, they're interested
in making purchasing decisions,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:06.250
not just by virtue of the quality of a
product, but what the company's all about,

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:10.200
what the company is trying to accomplish
and how they're trying to accomplish it.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:14.200
And regardless of whether or not the
company is making clear how they're going

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.210
about their business, people are going to
find out in this day and age.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:23.700
So I think purpose plays an important
role in terms of inspiration, motivation,

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:27.760
not only of your employees, but
also in terms of building a relationship

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:31.210
with your end customers and all the key
constituents within your ecosystem.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:37.100
&gt;&gt; So that purpose,
if I walk into an average office floor

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.520
in a LinkedIn building, and I walk in,
what are the cultural components?

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:44.020
What am I going to feel out
of the group that represents

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.640
the purpose that you just talked about,
of creating economic opportunity?

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:52.450
So you're going to get a sense,
I mean, if you literally ask people,

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:56.160
they would tell you we have five
cultural dimensions, we have six values.

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:00.370
And in summary,
if you ask people to define our culture,

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:02.510
they would say it's
a culture of transformation.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:05.180
Transformation of the self,
transformation of the company,

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:06.490
transformation of the world.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:12.030
The other four, integrity,
collaboration, humour and results.

00:24:13.718 --> 00:24:17.955
With regard to our values, the first
value that I'll tell you about and

00:24:17.955 --> 00:24:20.370
our most important value,
is that our members come first.

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:23.264
Not at the exclusion of
everything else we do.

00:24:23.264 --> 00:24:27.584
It's not members only, but it's members
first when we're making decisions,

00:24:27.584 --> 00:24:30.070
because without our
members we don't exist.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:37.280
Relationships matter and nested underneath
that is to manage compassionately.

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:40.540
To be open, honest, and constructive.

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.740
To demand excellence.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.780
To take intelligent risks and
to act like an owner.

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.230
And I would have a fairly
high degree of confidence.

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:55.201
That if you picked random people out
they'd be able to summarize that for

00:24:55.201 --> 00:24:56.924
you fairly accurately.

00:24:56.924 --> 00:25:01.510
And the reason for that is because as an
organization, we've made a commitment to

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:05.828
not just talk the talk, and put it up on
the walls and distribute the laminated

00:25:05.828 --> 00:25:10.220
cards that people put in their wallets,
but to walk the walk.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:14.340
And we do it through our recruiting
process and ensuring that people

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:17.920
are not just experienced, and
don’t just have the right skills, but

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:20.650
are also going to be a fit for
our organization.

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:24.720
We reinforce it on their first day
through the onboarding process.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:27.510
We develop against it through learning and
development, and

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:30.790
how continue to educate and
groom people throughout the organization.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:35.350
And we evaluate performance against it.

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:37.581
And this comes back to what we're
talking about with regard to purpose.

00:25:37.581 --> 00:25:41.380
That it's not just about the results
you get within our organization.

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:44.602
It's also about how you
went about doing it.

00:25:44.602 --> 00:25:47.645
&gt;&gt; And
it seems to some extent straightforward,

00:25:47.645 --> 00:25:50.538
when you're physically in the same space.

00:25:50.538 --> 00:25:53.116
And you can kind of have your finger
in the pots of what's happening But

00:25:53.116 --> 00:25:55.880
what about when you're bringing in
thousands of people across the globe?

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:56.600
How do you track it?

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:57.740
How do you make sure it's still there?

00:25:57.740 --> 00:25:59.350
&gt;&gt; It's an excellent question.

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:03.050
And the reason I'm smiling is I think
this is where a lot of hyper-growth

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:07.735
organizations have a tendency
to potentially go off the rails.

00:26:07.735 --> 00:26:10.363
The reason being, when you're in
hyper-growth mode, there is so

00:26:10.363 --> 00:26:12.079
much demand for your products or services.

00:26:12.079 --> 00:26:15.655
You're trying to keep up with that
demand to the best of your ability.

00:26:15.655 --> 00:26:18.845
And more often than not,
you have to hire a lot of people to do so.

00:26:18.845 --> 00:26:21.610
Whether it's people who are producing
the project, engineers,

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:23.496
product people designers, web devs, etc.

00:26:23.496 --> 00:26:24.648
Or the people selling the product.

00:26:24.648 --> 00:26:29.407
Or the people who we're supporting
the teams that are doing so, and for

00:26:29.407 --> 00:26:31.270
taking it to market, etc.

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:36.383
And so there's a natural inclination,
even if you've really spent the time and

00:26:36.383 --> 00:26:41.195
energy to define your mission,
codify your mission, codify your vision,

00:26:41.195 --> 00:26:43.624
codify your culture and your values.

00:26:43.624 --> 00:26:48.412
What you'll see from time to
time is people compromising on

00:26:48.412 --> 00:26:50.430
those things to keep up.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:53.994
And I'll give you an example of this,
an actual thing that happened.

00:26:53.994 --> 00:26:58.937
So early on in our hyper-growth mode
I remember sitting around a table

00:26:58.937 --> 00:27:01.549
with a number of senior executives.

00:27:01.549 --> 00:27:05.948
And we were evaluating the candidate
who was going to fill a very

00:27:05.948 --> 00:27:08.260
pressing need for us.

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:11.580
And as we were reviewing
their LinkedIn profile,

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.980
you didn't think I was
going to say resume, right?

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:17.390
As we were reviewing their LinkedIn
profile, the person who was

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:21.970
sponsoring this particular prospect said,
look at this background.

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:26.050
Look at the experience, the skill set,
the things they were involved with,

00:27:26.050 --> 00:27:28.350
the companies they worked for.

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:30.910
I would add that they're
not a cultural fit per se.

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:34.850
I have some concerns but
we'll take care of that.

00:27:35.990 --> 00:27:39.300
We'll coach him, we'll put this
person with the right mentor,

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:40.210
we'll make it happen.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.004
You know how often how that works out?

00:27:43.004 --> 00:27:47.086
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So it's very rare, very rare.

00:27:47.086 --> 00:27:49.143
There are exceptions, but

00:27:49.143 --> 00:27:54.250
seldom does someone change their
stripes in a situation like that.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:58.830
And what happens is,
exactly to the point of your question.

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:00.808
When you're all,
you start up a company and

00:28:00.808 --> 00:28:02.795
you're basically all under the same roof.

00:28:02.795 --> 00:28:04.099
You may all be in the same room.

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:08.960
And as you grow, maybe you're occupying
different floors within the same building.

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:10.877
It's still pretty easy to communicate,

00:28:10.877 --> 00:28:13.537
it's pretty easy to take
the pulse of the organization.

00:28:13.537 --> 00:28:15.499
You're going to hear about
things that aren't working out.

00:28:15.499 --> 00:28:17.140
You can get involved and fix them.

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:20.430
As soon as you start to really grow and
expand to multiple offices,

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.430
multiple cities, multiple countries.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:25.166
Imagine bringing that person
into your organization and

00:28:25.166 --> 00:28:29.273
having them lead a particular operation,
where you're not physically proximate.

00:28:29.273 --> 00:28:31.678
And they're bringing their own baggage,

00:28:31.678 --> 00:28:35.168
they're bringing what they
believe is the right thing to do.

00:28:35.168 --> 00:28:37.673
And you're not around
to help course correct.

00:28:37.673 --> 00:28:41.840
So they're now hiring people against what
they believe is the right thing to do.

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.630
And you're growing so fast, this dynamic

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:49.450
is taking place across multiple
functional areas and multiple cities.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:55.380
And before you know it, you're going to
have a very, very significant problem.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:57.840
So what you want to have
happen is what happened,

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:59.754
fast forward about six to nine months.

00:28:59.754 --> 00:29:03.020
Sitting around a similar table,
similar group of people,

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.990
evaluating a similarly
important candidate.

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:10.060
Person says, look at this experience,
look at these skills.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.780
Not a cultural fit,
let's move on to the next candidate.

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.457
And I was like, all right.

00:29:16.457 --> 00:29:17.721
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:17.721 --> 00:29:19.074
&gt;&gt; And where you're really

00:29:19.074 --> 00:29:20.041
on to something,

00:29:20.041 --> 00:29:24.940
is when that conversation starts taking
place without you being in the room.

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:28.030
And that's when you're on a path to
successfully scaling the talent within

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.440
your organization and your culture.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:33.160
&gt;&gt; You talk a lot about
managing compassionately,

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:34.980
you just referenced it in your answer.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:39.140
So what does that mean, and how does
it translate in your daily routine?

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:41.203
Just feel to see how that
looks on a daily basis.

00:29:41.203 --> 00:29:41.804
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:41.804 --> 00:29:45.935
So managing compassionately is
about putting yourself in another

00:29:45.935 --> 00:29:51.040
person's shoes and seeing the world
through their lens or perspective.

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:54.950
Classically defined, that's for the
purpose of alleviating their suffering.

00:29:54.950 --> 00:29:58.140
Within a business context I believe
it doesn't have to be limited to

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.160
alleviating suffering.

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:00.780
It can be helping them.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:02.530
Helping the team,
helping the organization.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:10.132
And day in and day out, whenever any
of us are dealing with other people,

00:30:10.132 --> 00:30:15.060
there are going to be times
where you're disagreeing.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:16.910
There's going to be conflict.

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:21.480
As a matter of fact, you probably had some
conflicts today that you could think of.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:24.800
And if you were to go to any company,
anywhere in the Valley, or

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:26.770
anywhere in the world for that matter.

00:30:26.770 --> 00:30:29.500
And you say, how many of you
have had some degree of conflict

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.390
throughout this working day?

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.755
You'd have a fair number of hands go up.

00:30:33.755 --> 00:30:38.535
And what typically happens when people
experience this conflict is one party,

00:30:38.535 --> 00:30:42.395
one person is going to start
to escalate emotionally.

00:30:42.395 --> 00:30:43.285
They're going to get angry,

00:30:43.285 --> 00:30:46.225
they're going to get defensive,
they're going to go on the attack.

00:30:46.225 --> 00:30:47.803
And more often than not,

00:30:47.803 --> 00:30:52.324
the person on the receiving end of
that may have n empathetic response.

00:30:52.324 --> 00:30:55.159
Which is to feel the anger that
the other person's feeling and

00:30:55.159 --> 00:30:57.040
become angry themselves.

00:30:57.040 --> 00:31:01.100
It's important, by the way, to distinguish
between empathy and compassion.

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:04.002
It's really important if you aspire
to managing compassionately.

00:31:04.002 --> 00:31:08.426
A lot of people, particularly in
western society, including myself 15,

00:31:08.426 --> 00:31:12.870
20 years ago, would use empathy and
compassion synonymously.

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:16.610
Empathy is feeling what
another person is feeling.

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:21.420
Fundamental building block to compassion.

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:25.980
Compassion is maintaining enough
distance where you can actually

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:29.160
do something about the way
the other person feels.

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:32.429
If you are strictly having
an empathetic response,

00:31:32.429 --> 00:31:34.962
to put in the parlance of the Dalai Lama.

00:31:34.962 --> 00:31:38.742
If you were walking along a trail in the
mountains and you came across a person who

00:31:38.742 --> 00:31:43.100
was being crushed by a boulder on their
chest, and they were suffocating.

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:46.168
The empathetic response would be for
you to suffocate as well.

00:31:46.168 --> 00:31:49.056
And if you're suffocating, you're no
longer in a position where you can help

00:31:49.056 --> 00:31:53.680
them The compassionate response is
to reflect on the fact that, not for

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:58.310
too long if they're suffocating, but to
understand that this person is suffering,

00:31:58.310 --> 00:32:02.060
and to do whatever you can
to alleviate that suffering,

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:03.496
get the boulder off of their chest.

00:32:03.496 --> 00:32:08.640
What does that mean, what does that
translate to in a work environment.

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:13.180
You're sitting there, you're in
a meeting and there's a disagreement.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:14.100
They'd get angry.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:16.060
You would normally get angry,

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:18.590
you would normally get defensive
if they're getting defensive.

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:20.440
And this goes back and forth.

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:23.290
And it becomes very
challenging to resolve.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:29.330
But what if, rather than just
blindly mirror their emotion,

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:34.240
or in other cases, often times people
just assume nefarious intention.

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.230
Well, how dare this person
disagree with me, or

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:40.390
attack my belief, my recommendation?

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:47.270
They must be political territorial,
they must be ignorant.

00:32:47.270 --> 00:32:49.959
As oppose to the compassionate response

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:55.000
being a spectator to your own thoughts and
especially your emotions.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.870
So taking a step outside yourself and
understanding what's going on.

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:01.920
Maybe that person is having a bad day and
it has nothing to do with you or

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:02.660
anything you said.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:09.100
Maybe you pushed a button in that
person where they had something

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:13.970
traumatic happen to them,
five years prior unbeknownst to you.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:17.470
Maybe you're talking about a subject that
the person is less familiar with and

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:19.190
they’re feeling a little bit vulnerable,

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:22.250
little bit insecure about looking
bad in front of their peers.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:26.340
I could go on and on with all
the potential reasons that this person

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:28.320
maybe acting the way that they are.

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:31.960
And the more you can take the time to
understand that, the more you can connect

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:37.090
with the person and constructively begin
to understand where they're coming from

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:41.480
and work towards compromise or the very
least work towards a shared understanding.

00:33:42.890 --> 00:33:44.620
That's managing compassionately.

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:48.320
And more often than not, especially
younger less experienced executive

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:51.080
like myself was ten, 12 years ago.

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:57.370
We have a tendency to expect everyone
around us to do things the way we do them.

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:00.370
That is natural.

00:34:00.370 --> 00:34:03.580
It's egocentric, not egomaniacal, okay?

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:05.470
It's egocentric and that's human nature.

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:09.610
But you're not going to get the best out
of the people you're working with if

00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:14.730
you're constantly holding
them up against a lens or

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:20.340
a bar of what you yourself are capable
of and precisely how you do things.

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:22.766
What makes far more sense,

00:34:22.766 --> 00:34:27.690
where you're going to get much more value,
out of those individuals in the team.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.530
Is when you understand, their strengths,
their unique strengths,

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:32.400
you play at their strengths.

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:34.740
Where you understand areas for
improvement, and

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:36.620
you coach them on those areas for
improvement, or

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:41.010
you change their role,such that it is
better aligned with their strengths.

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:43.420
And when you can do that,
when you can begin to do that,

00:34:43.420 --> 00:34:46.480
you start to unlock value
that's not possible.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.900
When you're only thinking about
the world through your own lens.

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:54.340
And so, managing compassionately has been
something I've been aspiring to do now

00:34:55.490 --> 00:34:58.210
since first realizing this.

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:01.700
And like I said, it's probably coming
up on 12 plus years at this point.

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:06.730
I say aspiring because it's really,
really hard to do.

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:11.420
Because we're all human and
we all have buttons that get pushed.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:14.940
So if anyone out there that decides they
do want to practice more compassionate

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:19.080
work, recognize you're not always going to
be able to do it and that's okay.

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:22.250
The aspirational component
that's really important.

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:25.010
&gt;&gt; I'm sure most of us after this talk
are going to empathized with you and

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:27.883
try to feel what it's like to
build a $26 billion company.

00:35:27.883 --> 00:35:32.639
The best, [LAUGH] that's promising.

00:35:32.639 --> 00:35:38.085
You're one of the very few CEO's that
survived multiple growth transitions.

00:35:38.085 --> 00:35:40.760
&gt;&gt; Hm-mm.
&gt;&gt; So went from private to public and

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:43.120
then through the Microsoft acquisition.

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:45.100
What keeps you the right man for the job?

00:35:45.100 --> 00:35:47.860
And what were the different challenges
that you had on each stage?

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:53.000
&gt;&gt; So in terms of the challenges
at each stage it's funny

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.970
you find the question that way.

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:58.320
I used to get asked after I first
join the company about six,

00:35:58.320 --> 00:35:59.470
nine months of being there.

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:00.360
What keeps me up at night?

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:05.870
And, beyond having a newborn at the time,

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:10.160
which was keeping us up,
literally keeping us up at night.

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:14.740
In terms of the work, it wasn't
keeping me up at night, per se, but

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:22.320
what I constantly and vigilantly focused
on was the idea of focus and scale.

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:26.480
That was the case when I first join
the organization we had 338 people,

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:29.120
that's the case today we had
more than 10,000 people.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:30.520
Focus and scale.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:36.920
Focus is understanding not just
your mission and vision and

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:40.540
your culture and your values but
your core value proposition.

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:45.900
What is the value that your going to bring
to the people that you serve your members,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.360
your customers, etcetera.

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:53.660
And all too often particularly
among its hyper road companies.

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:57.790
You see organizations that break on
something that introducing new prior to

00:36:57.790 --> 00:37:01.660
service and there's massive,
massive demand.

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:05.490
And they go on this hyper growth journey,
but they don't necessarily take the time

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:11.780
to codify and really specify
their core value proposition.

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:15.220
If you want to visualise,
think about a target, and

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:18.610
the bullseye is your core value
proposition, the manifestation,

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:22.050
the product or service that's going to be
the clearest manifestation of your core.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:25.140
Value proposition, our core value
proposition is to connect our members and

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:26.430
customers to opportunity.

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:31.740
And when you draw this target,
you start with that bull's eye.

00:37:31.740 --> 00:37:35.590
And then you can add concentric circles,
and those concentric circles are what's

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:41.940
possible, the adjacencies that
are possible as you extend from your core.

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:44.510
If companies do take
the time to define the core,

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:48.070
and that's one of the classic mistakes
is they don't take the time to do so.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:52.850
But if they take the time to define
the core, the next most common

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:58.020
mistake I see in hyper-growth situation,
is to draw resources away form the core

00:37:58.020 --> 00:38:02.050
too quickly in pursuit of
the next bright shiny object.

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:06.140
And as the result of that you
leave your core vulnerable.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:09.290
And if you had success there's going to
be a lot of competition waiting for

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:10.470
you to stumble.

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:12.640
And they've seen what you've done, and

00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:16.510
there going after your core and
then they go after the core.

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:19.200
And you're in a position where you
have to react to the competition now.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:21.780
And you have to draw resources
away from that growth opportunity,

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:22.380
back to your core.

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:24.510
And you're whip selling people
within your organization.

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:28.160
Now you're reacting as opposed to
being proactive and strategic and

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:28.860
that is no good.

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:35.200
So, clearly define and
codify your core, make sure

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:40.100
your first managerial cycle, your first
dollar of capital, your first resources

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:43.734
are going into the support of building
that core and achieving critical mass.

00:38:43.734 --> 00:38:47.800
And once you achieve critical mass,
then take incremental investment and

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:51.270
invest it into strategic opportunities and

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:53.159
what we like to call venture plays or
venture bets.

00:38:54.760 --> 00:38:57.100
So that focus is critical.

00:38:57.100 --> 00:39:00.920
It's also really important that people
take the time to understand skill.

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:06.970
And again with regard to hyper growth
companies, a lot of organizations and

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:11.060
the people leading this organizations
become fixated on innovation.

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:15.240
And at times that happens at the exclusion

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:19.490
of putting the right building blocks
in place, to support scale over time.

00:39:20.620 --> 00:39:25.970
And scale,
Brastax comes from less glamorous things.

00:39:25.970 --> 00:39:30.330
It comes from investing the right people
in talent, it comes from investing in

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:34.129
the right process, it comes from
investing in the right infrastructure.

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:39.060
And that will lead to future
innovation and success.

00:39:39.060 --> 00:39:43.420
But if your focused on innovation at the
exclusion of those things where you're not

00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:46.990
taking the time and energy in prioritizing
those elements, those foundational

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:51.750
building blocks, it's going to be very,
very difficult to scale the organization.

00:39:51.750 --> 00:39:52.550
Over time.

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:57.520
The last thing I would add, if we're
talking about a company in terms of

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:02.260
multiple phases, is as an organization
matures and it comes out of hyper growth,

00:40:02.260 --> 00:40:04.920
which inevitably happens to
virtually all organizations,

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:08.875
although if you look at Facebook's most
recent results, they're still growing 50%,

00:40:08.875 --> 00:40:13.110
$32 billion revenue run rate,
which kind of defies gravity.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:13.660
Very unusual.

00:40:15.690 --> 00:40:19.710
But as organizations mature and
their growth rates slow,

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:23.240
it's really important to
get ahead of that dynamic.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.840
And that starts by being
very honest with yourself

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:30.190
about your addressable opportunity.

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:34.980
And I think all too often,
companies kind of get a little too

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:40.920
fixated on the marketing material and
how they position the organization,

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:42.520
what the company is capable of doing,

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:46.800
and they're not necessarily honest with
themselves about the real addressable.

00:40:47.860 --> 00:40:52.720
And if organizations just use these
theoretical numbers and constructs, and

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:57.357
they don't really look under the hood in
terms of where saturation is going to

00:40:57.357 --> 00:41:00.963
occur, you're going to end up
reacting to slowing growth,

00:41:00.963 --> 00:41:03.634
as opposed to proactively addressing it.

00:41:03.634 --> 00:41:08.610
And ideally, laying the ground work and
layering an investment that

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:14.660
enables you to add the next S curve
before that growth decline begins.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:19.240
And that is absolutely critical
towards sustainable growth.

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:23.402
And you see it amongst the modern
tech titans increasingly.

00:41:23.402 --> 00:41:26.914
These organizations making huge
investments beyond the core,

00:41:26.914 --> 00:41:30.368
and they're being very proactive,
very strategic about it.

00:41:30.368 --> 00:41:36.560
And that continues to help them sustain
growth and unbelievable levels of skill.

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:38.909
&gt;&gt; That sounds like, of course,
that we should have you in the GSB.

00:41:38.909 --> 00:41:39.952
Yeah, just pulling it out there.

00:41:39.952 --> 00:41:42.847
&gt;&gt; Well, Reed,
in all seriousness, Reed and

00:41:42.847 --> 00:41:46.699
a few friends of his taught
a course here on blitz scaling.

00:41:46.699 --> 00:41:50.018
When they talked about how the scaling or
the dean is going, yes, he did.

00:41:50.018 --> 00:41:52.062
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:41:52.062 --> 00:41:59.642
&gt;&gt; The LinkedIn team just or the LinkedIn
team built a $26.2 billion company.

00:41:59.642 --> 00:42:02.720
The largest acquisition that
Microsoft has ever made.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:05.518
It's quite an accomplishment,
so, congratulations.

00:42:05.518 --> 00:42:06.192
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:42:06.192 --> 00:42:09.640
&gt;&gt; How do you envision the future
with this partnership?

00:42:09.640 --> 00:42:10.627
What are they going to change?

00:42:10.627 --> 00:42:15.600
&gt;&gt; So, in terms of change,
I'll tell you what it doesn't change.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:17.770
It doesn't change our sense of purpose.

00:42:17.770 --> 00:42:19.160
It doesn't change our vision or

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:22.890
our mission, and that was critical
to getting the deal done.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:23.941
So, when Satya and

00:42:23.941 --> 00:42:27.751
I first sat down before the deal was
announced, we both agreed that we

00:42:27.751 --> 00:42:32.234
would have to have at least two dimensions
in place in terms of strong alignment.

00:42:32.234 --> 00:42:35.230
One was sense of purpose and
the other was structure.

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:39.680
And with regard to sense of purpose,
it turns out that our mission,

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:43.525
that worded differently than Microsoft's
mission, to empower every individual and

00:42:43.525 --> 00:42:46.820
organization on the planet to achieve
more, are actually quite similar.

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:50.750
Different wording, and
we have gone about it differently.

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:52.160
We built a professional network and

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:55.030
we're increasingly investing in building
out the world's first economic graph.

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:58.390
Microsoft, historically through software
and increasingly through the cloud.

00:42:58.390 --> 00:43:01.315
But we're trying to
accomplish the same thing.

00:43:01.315 --> 00:43:03.840
Satya is extremely purpose driven.

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.780
And Microsoft, under his watch,
has become an increasingly purpose driven,

00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:07.615
it's very important to him.

00:43:07.615 --> 00:43:09.730
So, we had alignment there.

00:43:09.730 --> 00:43:11.240
So, you could check that box.

00:43:11.240 --> 00:43:13.886
And then, we turned in that
discussion to structure, and

00:43:13.886 --> 00:43:15.290
I didn't know what to expect.

00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:18.300
I didn't know what he was going to say.

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:21.172
Was he going to ask me to
move up to Redmond and for

00:43:21.172 --> 00:43:24.570
the organization to relocate?

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:29.560
And he said, so, I think, in this case,
it's really important that LinkedIn

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:34.700
continues to be independent,
and he had made independence.

00:43:34.700 --> 00:43:40.095
So, we were talking about what
Facebook had done with WhatsApp and

00:43:40.095 --> 00:43:42.040
Oculus, and of course, Instagram.

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:44.215
What Google has been able
to do with YouTube, and

00:43:44.215 --> 00:43:48.030
increasingly you see these
organizations at enormous scale.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:53.365
Being able to acquire companies and really
preserve what has made those companies

00:43:53.365 --> 00:43:58.170
successful, and allow those companies
to continue to build off of that.

00:43:58.170 --> 00:44:02.845
But at the same time, leveraging
the broader assets of the organization.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:05.511
And that is very much what Satya and

00:44:05.511 --> 00:44:07.967
I were hoping to accomplish
with this combination.

00:44:07.967 --> 00:44:11.710
So, LinkedIn continues to pursue
the same mission and vision.

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:15.250
We continue to do so in a way that
manifests our culture and values.

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:16.384
We have the same leadership.

00:44:16.384 --> 00:44:20.879
We're doing the same work, but we have
an opportunity now to leverage Microsoft's

00:44:20.879 --> 00:44:24.950
customer footprint of over a billion
individuals that use their software.

00:44:26.140 --> 00:44:30.470
And to leverage their extraordinary
investment in advanced technology.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:32.344
And to leverage all of that talent.

00:44:32.344 --> 00:44:36.220
And to accelerate the realization
of our mission and vision.

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:37.150
And that's the hope.

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:42.772
&gt;&gt; So, we have one final question
before we move on to the Q and A.

00:44:42.772 --> 00:44:46.070
You mentioned interview with
Oprah that in your early 30s

00:44:46.070 --> 00:44:50.695
you had a bit of a very fundamental
change on your perspectives on life.

00:44:50.695 --> 00:44:51.330
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:52.280
&gt;&gt; And that led to a better you.

00:44:52.280 --> 00:44:55.170
I would say that it is fair to
say that most of us around here

00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:59.310
are still in search of that
better version of ourselves.

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:01.880
So, can you tell us a bit more
about that transition and

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:05.470
any pitfalls that we shouldn't or
should watch out from?

00:45:05.470 --> 00:45:06.667
&gt;&gt; Dude, you went all Oprah on me.

00:45:06.667 --> 00:45:08.083
I mean, that's a full-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:08.083 --> 00:45:08.861
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:08.861 --> 00:45:10.188
&gt;&gt; You went full Oprah,

00:45:10.188 --> 00:45:12.994
who did an amazing job,
by the way, up here.

00:45:12.994 --> 00:45:13.633
&gt;&gt; On this stage.

00:45:13.633 --> 00:45:16.132
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I have seen that interview,

00:45:16.132 --> 00:45:17.163
she was fantastic.

00:45:17.163 --> 00:45:21.770
So, you may be referring to an interview
I did with Oprah on Super Soul Sunday.

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:23.230
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:25.120
&gt;&gt; And she was asking,

00:45:25.120 --> 00:45:28.070
how many of you watch Super Soul Sunday,
any Super Soulers?

00:45:29.210 --> 00:45:31.089
Will, you better get your hand up man.

00:45:31.089 --> 00:45:34.604
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Will didn't even, thank you, Will.

00:45:34.604 --> 00:45:41.280
So, Oprah had done some research and
done her homework for the interview.

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:47.140
And she said, in reading one
particular profile in the, that

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:53.355
I had been known in a prior role before
I got to LinkedIn to be very intense.

00:45:53.355 --> 00:45:57.480
[LAUGH] Which I think would be
putting it somewhat euphemistically.

00:45:57.480 --> 00:46:00.625
And I am intense and I was intense.

00:46:00.625 --> 00:46:04.804
We talked a little bit about my management
practices at the time were I was expecting

00:46:04.804 --> 00:46:07.984
people to do things the way I did them and
I would go frustrated.

00:46:07.984 --> 00:46:10.471
And it's not like yelling and
throwing things frustrated, but

00:46:10.471 --> 00:46:11.538
frustrated nonetheless.

00:46:11.538 --> 00:46:14.278
Different management style
though when I have to.

00:46:14.278 --> 00:46:18.523
And she said what lead to you
managing compassionately and

00:46:18.523 --> 00:46:21.330
what part of your practice?

00:46:21.330 --> 00:46:26.470
And I told her about some specific things
that had occurred within my role at

00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:27.570
the time.

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:30.230
But at the end of the day,
the biggest difference for

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:32.056
me personally was meeting my wife.

00:46:32.056 --> 00:46:32.682
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:46:32.682 --> 00:46:39.490
&gt;&gt; And my wife led to a very
different perspective for me.

00:46:39.490 --> 00:46:45.280
And she valued things that I hadn't
placed as much value on historically.

00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:50.134
Things like kindness, and love, and

00:46:50.134 --> 00:46:56.207
gratitude, and
it changed everything for me.

00:46:56.207 --> 00:46:58.808
And I'm very,
very fortunate to have met here.

00:46:58.808 --> 00:47:00.665
And you hear all the time,

00:47:00.665 --> 00:47:04.903
I wouldn't be in this position
if it weren't for so and so.

00:47:04.903 --> 00:47:08.723
But in my case, it's absolutely the case.

00:47:08.723 --> 00:47:10.685
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that.

00:47:10.685 --> 00:47:11.288
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:47:11.288 --> 00:47:12.763
&gt;&gt; I'm going to open up the Q and A now.

00:47:21.145 --> 00:47:23.062
There we go.

00:47:23.062 --> 00:47:25.750
&gt;&gt; Jeff, thanks for being here and
for your candour today.

00:47:25.750 --> 00:47:28.680
My name's Holly May,
I'm an alum from the class of 2016.

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:31.000
I want to go back to where
we started the dialogue.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:37.890
And as you think about as an executive and
as a visible person in the valley,

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:42.620
how do you think about which topics
to speak on and which not to?

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:45.820
And in the current context,
we've seen a lot of execs talk about

00:47:45.820 --> 00:47:50.247
the Executive orders and yet
these weeks starts Black History month and

00:47:50.247 --> 00:47:54.293
that hasn't gotten an equal amount of
attention, how do you think about which

00:47:54.293 --> 00:47:58.172
topics to show up on and which not to and
the consequences of those choices?

00:47:58.172 --> 00:48:02.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so interestingly enough
we have a lot of activity

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:06.029
plan within LinkedIn on
Black History month.

00:48:06.029 --> 00:48:10.350
And it's been led by an amazing employee,
resource group and

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:14.100
our leadership there and
we're excited about that.

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:19.370
You asked an excellent question and

00:48:19.370 --> 00:48:23.795
my first response would be
there is no formula for this.

00:48:23.795 --> 00:48:27.771
There's no cookie cutter approach to this.

00:48:27.771 --> 00:48:33.648
It, at times, is a deeply personal
decision, I think it's really important

00:48:33.648 --> 00:48:39.179
to know where you stand, if you're
going to be in a position of leadership.

00:48:39.179 --> 00:48:42.404
To know what your company
is trying to accomplish,

00:48:42.404 --> 00:48:44.804
to know how it wants to accomplish it,

00:48:44.804 --> 00:48:49.380
to be very clear with people about
those values and where you draw lines.

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:52.260
And where you're willing to compromise and
where you're not.

00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:58.540
And when those lines get crossed,
to do something about it,

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:00.460
to speak up, to take action.

00:49:01.810 --> 00:49:07.339
And at the same time to recognize
that your personal beliefs maybe at

00:49:07.339 --> 00:49:13.938
odds with the personal beliefs of people
that worked within your organization.

00:49:13.938 --> 00:49:20.014
And that as long as people who disagree
do so in a mutually respectful and

00:49:20.014 --> 00:49:24.775
compassionate and
constructive way that is consistent

00:49:24.775 --> 00:49:30.360
with the way in which you want
to go about your business.

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:34.707
Consistent with your culture and
consistent with your values, that every

00:49:34.707 --> 00:49:38.925
individual within an organisation
should feel as if they belong there.

00:49:38.925 --> 00:49:42.993
So that's the approach that I've taken,
that we take as a leadership team.

00:49:42.993 --> 00:49:46.243
But it's going to be different depending
on who you're talking to, depending

00:49:46.243 --> 00:49:49.552
on what they're trying to accomplish,
depending on their own experiences.

00:50:02.403 --> 00:50:05.840
Thank you so much for being here and
sharing all the information with us.

00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:08.270
And I'm Akasha and
I'm an undergrad freshman.

00:50:08.270 --> 00:50:09.770
Two real quick questions.

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:14.270
So first one is what kind of innovation
do you think is happening at LinkedIn and

00:50:14.270 --> 00:50:18.880
what innovation are you
concentrating more on?

00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:23.240
And what advice to you give for
undergrads who want

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:27.739
to start their companies?

00:50:27.739 --> 00:50:32.043
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Have a good LinkedIn profile?

00:50:32.043 --> 00:50:33.085
&gt;&gt; Okay, yeah.

00:50:33.085 --> 00:50:37.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'm the first in terms

00:50:37.170 --> 00:50:39.490
of the innovation taking place.

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:40.946
We are, last year,

00:50:40.946 --> 00:50:45.417
we completely overhauled our
mobile flagship application.

00:50:45.417 --> 00:50:48.349
We are in the process right now
of rolling out a new version of

00:50:48.349 --> 00:50:53.170
our desktop experience, that's build on a
new front end framework called Pemberley.

00:50:53.170 --> 00:50:56.340
That's going to enable us to iterate and

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:59.870
innovate up to ten times faster
than what we could do historically.

00:50:59.870 --> 00:51:02.862
So we've got some very strong
building blocks in place.

00:51:02.862 --> 00:51:06.110
We also have a very clear sense of
the assets that we've been able to

00:51:06.110 --> 00:51:07.530
develop today.

00:51:07.530 --> 00:51:10.460
One of the things that we're
going to be investing in

00:51:10.460 --> 00:51:15.350
more aggressively is the application
of artificial intelligence.

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:20.150
Both LinkedIn and Microsoft have
amazing talent along those lines, and

00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:22.810
Microsoft has invested
very heavily in this area.

00:51:22.810 --> 00:51:26.690
And we can bring that to bear
on LinkedIn through things like

00:51:26.690 --> 00:51:31.370
a bot experience that you could think
of as your own personal assistant.

00:51:31.370 --> 00:51:35.290
So we could provide a personal
professional assistant to every member of

00:51:35.290 --> 00:51:38.140
LinkedIn, whether that's
calendar integration and

00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:40.790
providing you the information you
need to be better prepared for

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:42.680
the person you're about to meet.

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:46.890
Or ultimately making sure you have access
to the right information to complete

00:51:46.890 --> 00:51:48.260
a project.

00:51:48.260 --> 00:51:51.848
There's a whole host of things that we're
very excited about to bring to bear there.

00:51:51.848 --> 00:51:55.702
We're going to continue to invest very
aggressively in making our feed and

00:51:55.702 --> 00:52:00.310
the content within out feed more
relevant and more personally relevant.

00:52:00.310 --> 00:52:02.340
We've only begun to scratch
the surface there, and

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:05.270
what you're going to start to see this
year are what we call Interest Feeds.

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:09.180
So we can take packages of content
by virtue of machine learning,

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:13.780
social queues, and our editorial team and
we can curate these packages.

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:18.820
So whether it's around the executive order
or a major announcement by your company,

00:52:20.020 --> 00:52:25.130
or a shift in the economy, you can not
only see articles that were written

00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:29.410
by publishers that you're following, or
influencers that you're following, but

00:52:29.410 --> 00:52:31.310
what people in your network are saying,
and

00:52:31.310 --> 00:52:35.330
the information you need to know right now
as professional to make better decisions.

00:52:35.330 --> 00:52:39.860
So we've only begin to scratch the surface
there, so stay tuned on that front.

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:45.368
We are going to be starting to think
about, how and this is somewhat consistent

00:52:45.368 --> 00:52:50.628
with second part of your question,
how we can create stronger marketplace

00:52:50.628 --> 00:52:56.270
dynamics with regard to opportunities
that our membership is seeking.

00:52:56.270 --> 00:53:00.710
Not just jobs per se, although that's a
huge priority for us, but say mentors and

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:06.020
mentees or people that want to get
involved in philanthropic activities and

00:53:06.020 --> 00:53:10.900
the non profits who could use their
skill sets or their resources.

00:53:10.900 --> 00:53:14.687
And for those seeking investment,
if you're starting a company,

00:53:14.687 --> 00:53:18.953
we've got a lot of people on LinkedIn
who are very interested in investing.

00:53:18.953 --> 00:53:23.086
And those are just a few examples of what
would be possible with regard to those

00:53:23.086 --> 00:53:25.830
marketplace dynamics,
so stay tuned for that.

00:53:25.830 --> 00:53:29.270
We're going to start to
experiment with that as well.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:33.479
And that's largely [INAUDIBLE] from
LinkedIn and there's number of areas where

00:53:33.479 --> 00:53:37.768
we're very excited to potentially
integrate assets with regard to Microsoft.

00:53:37.768 --> 00:53:44.304
So bringing in LinkedIn, LinkedIn Identity
your network into the Office experience,

00:53:44.304 --> 00:53:49.798
notifications within Windows,
we're excited about the opportunity.

00:53:49.798 --> 00:53:54.228
Microsoft Word is still the primary
resource people use to create resumes, and

00:53:54.228 --> 00:53:57.797
you can imagine the day where
you're creating your resume, and

00:53:57.797 --> 00:54:02.225
then a right rail appears, which asks you
if you want to integrate that resume at

00:54:02.225 --> 00:54:06.720
the click of a button into Linked In,
refresh your profile, create a profile for

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:11.060
the first time, and then we can recommend
jobs for you within that context.

00:54:11.060 --> 00:54:14.210
So there's some exciting things we're
doing along those lines as well.

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:21.310
In terms of advice, I would give to you if
you're interested in starting a company,

00:54:21.310 --> 00:54:25.380
I guess it wouldn't be to similar from the
advice I would give anyone in the audience

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:32.360
in terms of how to optimize for their
career path and how to achieve success.

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:36.033
And that would be first and foremost take
the time to understand what it is you're

00:54:36.033 --> 00:54:37.664
ultimately trying to accomplish.

00:54:37.664 --> 00:54:42.557
And the more specific you can be,
the more likely you will be to achieve it,

00:54:42.557 --> 00:54:46.769
and you're going to manifest that
in ways explicit and implicit.

00:54:46.769 --> 00:54:50.240
And I don't mean this in some
new agey predeterministic way,

00:54:50.240 --> 00:54:52.360
I mean very practically.

00:54:52.360 --> 00:54:55.990
The clearer you are about what it
is that you want to accomplish,

00:54:55.990 --> 00:54:59.720
the more you can clearly communicate
that to the people around you.

00:54:59.720 --> 00:55:01.060
The more doors are going to open up,

00:55:01.060 --> 00:55:04.430
the more you're going to attract
the right kind of opportunities.

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:05.286
So that's really important.

00:55:05.286 --> 00:55:07.429
And when it comes to defining what it is,

00:55:07.429 --> 00:55:10.833
you ought to know what you want to
accomplish, try to optimize for

00:55:10.833 --> 00:55:15.140
two things, your passion and your skill,
not one at the exclusion of the other.

00:55:15.140 --> 00:55:16.450
So that'll be first.

00:55:16.450 --> 00:55:20.810
Second, would be to surround yourself with
the best possible talent that you can.

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:26.525
And when I was younger, I used to think
that was all about itching my wagon's

00:55:26.525 --> 00:55:29.244
the right train in terms of my boss or
mentor.

00:55:29.244 --> 00:55:34.468
And as I gotten older, I've realized
that is the world becomes more network,

00:55:34.468 --> 00:55:39.297
as we become more dependent on knowledge,
having the right knowledge at

00:55:39.297 --> 00:55:44.625
the right time to accomplish certain
objectives, it's really about people.

00:55:44.625 --> 00:55:49.531
And so it's not just about who you
work for, it's about who you work

00:55:49.531 --> 00:55:54.955
alongside of and who works for you and
making sure that you don't settle and

00:55:54.955 --> 00:55:59.949
you don't compromise, in terms of
where you set that bar on skill and

00:55:59.949 --> 00:56:02.820
talent and on fit with your own values.

00:56:04.050 --> 00:56:05.818
I think that's really important.

00:56:05.818 --> 00:56:11.221
Personally, I like to work with people
who dream big, who, can we curse here,

00:56:11.221 --> 00:56:16.480
is that all right, okay, who get shit done
[LAUGH] and who know how to have fun.

00:56:16.480 --> 00:56:19.186
Not to be confused with constantly
partying because it's going to be

00:56:19.186 --> 00:56:22.050
difficult to get work done if
you're constantly partying.

00:56:22.050 --> 00:56:26.650
But knowing how to have fun as a proxy for
being of like minds culturally.

00:56:26.650 --> 00:56:28.600
So surround yourself with
only the best talent.

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:31.940
And the third would be
to always be learning.

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:35.460
The world is changing so fast right now.

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:38.700
There's an acceleration, it's like log or
rhythmic, the rate of innovation.

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:43.566
And the days where you could study
one thing and have a role or

00:56:43.566 --> 00:56:48.528
a job guaranteed for the rest of your
life, I think those days are long gone.

00:56:48.528 --> 00:56:52.236
We have to constantly be learning,
continuously learning.

00:56:52.236 --> 00:56:57.512
And if you have a natural inclination or
passion for that,

00:56:57.512 --> 00:57:02.787
if you're naturally curious
about the world around you,

00:57:02.787 --> 00:57:09.261
you will be that much better positioned
to continue to learn over time.

00:57:09.261 --> 00:57:15.789
&gt;&gt; We're not Oprah's soul Sundays but
we hope this was fun for you.

00:57:15.789 --> 00:57:17.727
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was great.

00:57:17.727 --> 00:57:19.767
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:57:19.767 --> 00:57:20.663
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks for having me.

00:57:20.663 --> 00:57:25.960
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
[MUSIC]

